{
  "title": "rPPG-Toolbox \u2014 healthcare (1,078\u2605)",
  "content": "Project: rPPG-Toolbox\nDomain: healthcare | Scale: medium | Stars: 1,078\nProblem: Camera-based remote photoplethysmography (rPPG) lacks a unified benchmarking platform for comparing traditional and deep learning physiological sensing algorithms. rPPG-Toolbox provides a standardized open-source framework to benchmark state-of-the-art methods and support rapid development of new algorithms for contactless vital sign measurement.\nTarget users: Academic researchers in computer vision and healthcare AI, Machine learning engineers working on physiological sensing, Healthcare technology developers, Mobile health application developers, PhD students and scientists benchmarking rPPG algorithms\nCore features: Benchmarking of multiple traditional unsupervised rPPG algorithms (GREEN, ICA, CHROM, LGI, PBV, POS, OMIT); Benchmarking of supervised deep learning rPPG models (DeepPhys, PhysNet, TS-CAN, EfficientPhys, PhysFormer, BigSmall, PhysMamba, RhythmFormer, FactorizePhys, iBVPNet); Support for multiple physiological datasets (SCAMPS, UBFC-rPPG, PURE, BP4D+, UBFC-Phys, MMPD, iBVP); Configurable training and evaluation pipeline via YAML configuration files; Custom algorithm development scaffolding for rapid prototyping; CUDA-accelerated model inference and training; Jupyter Notebook-based experimentation and visualization; TensorBoard training monitoring and metrics logging\nIntegrations: TensorBoardX, OpenCV, NeuroKit2, Mamba SSM, timm (PyTorch Image Models)\nTech stack: Jupyter Notebook + Python + CUDA/GPU compute\nKey libraries: PyTorch, OpenCV, NumPy, SciPy, scikit-learn, scikit-image, timm, mamba-ssm, causal-conv1d, NeuroKit2, TensorBoardX, h5py, yacs, pandas, matplotlib, thop\nArchitecture: monolith\nDesign lessons:\n- YAML-based configuration management (via yacs) enables flexible experiment reproducibility without code changes, a common pattern in ML research toolboxes.\n- Unified dataset abstraction layer supporting multiple dataset formats (.mat via h5py/mat73) allows seamless swapping of training/evaluation datasets across all models.\n- Mixing traditional signal-processing baselines with modern deep learning models (including Transformer and Mamba-based architectures) in the same framework enables fair apples-to-apples benchmarking.\n- Custom CUDA/C++ extensions alongside Python indicate performance-critical preprocessing or signal extraction routines are offloaded to native code for efficiency.",
